 "equations": [
  {
   "name": "Logic function",
   "expr": "Y = A + B  (equivalently Y = NOT(NOT A · NOT B))",
   "where": {
    "A": "input A",
    "B": "input B",
    "Y": "output, positive logic"
   },
   "note": "",
   "page": 1
  }
 ],
 "operation": [
  {
   "topic": "Function",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "Y is driven high whenever either input is high, and low only when both inputs are low."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Input levels and translation",
   "page": 8,
   "text": "The inputs switch at TTL levels (high above 2 V, low below 0.8 V), so a signal from 3.3 V logic is read correctly while the part runs from 5 V, and the output then swings the full 5 V. The inputs present a high impedance when VCC is 0 V. An input sitting at a TTL high level such as 3.4 V, rather than at a rail, raises supply current by up to 1.5 mA per input."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Output edges",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "The output stage has low drive and slowed rise and fall times, which keeps overshoot, undershoot and ringing down on bus lines. Drive is balanced between source and sink."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Design limits",
   "page": 9,
   "text": "Input edges should stay within the 20 ns/V transition rate, and input levels within VIH and VIL. Keep load current to 25 mA per output and 50 mA in total through the part, never pull the output above VCC, and avoid bus contention, since the output can push currents beyond its ratings. Consider trace routing and load, since fast edges into a light load can ring."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Power supply and bypassing",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "Any supply from 4.5 V to 5.5 V works. Bypass VCC with 0.1 µF placed as close to the pin as possible; 0.1 µF and 1 µF in parallel may be used to cover different noise frequencies."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Unused inputs",
   "page": 10,
   "text": "An input must never be left floating: an open CMOS input settles at an undefined voltage and the logic state becomes undefined. Tie an unused input to VCC or to GND, whichever suits the function. An unused output may be left open."
  },
  {
   "topic": "ESD and latch-up",
   "page": 1,
   "text": "Every pin withstands ±2000 V HBM and ±1000 V CDM. Latch-up immunity exceeds 250 mA per JESD 17."
  },
  {
   "topic": "Package and mounting",
   "page": 16,
   "text": "All three packages are MSL level 1 at 260 °C peak reflow. Body dimensions exclude mold flash, protrusions and gate burrs, which add at most 0.25 mm per side on DBV and DCK and 0.15 mm per side on DRL. The DBV and DCK bodies may carry a small support pin on the side opposite pin 2, or none. DBV follows JEDEC MO-178, DCK follows JEDEC MO-203 (its lead width does not), and DRL follows JEDEC MO-293 variation UAAD-1."
  }
 ],
